 { Zbynek Hracek – Miso Cebalo, Medulin 2002 Learning objective: This game illustrates the power of Black’s exchange sacrifice ...Rxc3. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Qa5 ( { The line } 10... Rc8 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. Kb1 Nc4 13. Bxc4 Rxc4 { is far more common. } ) 
11. Bb3 Rfc8 12. h4 Ne5 13. h5 Rxc3 $5 ( { PH: Black could consider taking the pawn, since after } 13... Nxh5 14. g4 Nf6 15. Bh6 Bh8 { it's not clear how the attack should continue. However, } 14. Nd5 $1 Qxd2 15. Rxd2 { actually leads to an unpleasant endgame for Black: } Kf8 16. g4 Nf6 17. Nxf6 Bxf6 18. Rdh2 $14 { White regains the pawn with a small edge. } ) 
14. bxc3 $6 { We are essentially outside of theory, though the exchange sacrifice is a very typical resource in this opening. White has to deal with the burden of the doubled c-pawns, which in many variations prevent him from carrying out a decisive kingside attack. The sacrifice is positionally and tactically well justified. } ( { If } 14. Qxc3 Qxc3 15. bxc3 Nxh5 16. g4 ( { However, instead of } 16. g4 { , the direct } 16. Nf5 $1 { opens up the position favourably after } gxf5 17. Rxh5 fxe4 18. Bd5 exf3 19. gxf3 $14 { when Black is still in the game, but he does not quite have a fortress anymore. } ) 
Nf6 { there are no weaknesses for White to attack, for example } 17. Bh6 a5 18. a3 Rc8 19. Kb2 b5 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. Rh4 h5 $5 { when Black is rock-solid. } ) 
 ( { PH: In the above line, after } 16. g4 Nf6 { the computer wants to continue } 17. g5 Nh5 { , simply sidelining Black's knight for the forseeable future. Play could continue } 18. Kd2 b5 19. Rb1 { , and now for instance } a5 $6 { is answered by } 20. a4 $1 bxa4 21. Bd5 { followed by Rb7 with a clear advantage. } ) 
Nxh5 15. Bh6 Bxh6 16. Qxh6 Nf6 $1 { Preventing a potential Rxh5 sacrifice. } ( { Instead } 16... Qxc3 { could be met by } 17. Ne2 $1 Qc5 18. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 19. Rh1 { and the attack forces Black to give up a piece with } Ng4 20. fxg4 Bxg4 21. Nf4 { when White should be able to win this. PH: After } 17. Ne2 { a funny line is } Nd3 18. Rxd3 Qa1 19. Kd2 Qxh1 20. g4 Ng3 21. Qxh1 Nxh1 22. Ke3 $16 { and Black's knight is trapped on h1. } ) 
17. Kb2 $6 ( { If } 17. g4 Qxc3 18. Kb1 ( { but not } 18. g5 $4 Qa1 $1 19. Kd2 Qxd4 $19 ) 
Rc8 19. Nf5 $5 ( { again not } 19. g5 $2 Nh5 20. Rxh5 gxh5 21. Rh1 Qxd4 22. Qxh5 Nc4 23. Qxh7 Kf8 $19 ) 
Bxf5 20. exf5 Nc4 21. Qc1 Qxf3 $15 { when Black has three pawns for the exchange and his king is quite safe. } ) 
 ( { Better was } 17. Kb1 $1 { and after } Qxc3 18. Ne2 { if Black chooses } Qc5 ( { Instead } 18. Kb2 Qc8 $1 19. Nf4 Qf8 { gives Black a solid position. } ) 
19. Nf4 $1 { ± the threat of 20.Nd5 gets Black into serious trouble. } ) 
 ( { Also plausible is } 17. Ne2 $5 { to follow up with Nf4-d5. } ) 
Rc8 18. Qe3 $6 { What is interesting about Black’s exchange sacrifice is that White is unable to hold his queenside through passive defence. } ( { Better was } 18. Kb1 $1 { and after } Rc5 19. Ne2 Bb5 20. Nf4 { White can create some threats too. } ) 
Qc5 19. Rh4 a5 $1 { ∓ White is already in big trouble, as his bishop is misplaced and his king unsafe. } 20. Rdh1 ( 20. a3 { is answered by: } a4 21. Ba2 Be6 $1 { Gaining the crucial c4-square. } 22. Bxe6 ( { not } 22. Nxe6 $4 Qxe3 ) 
fxe6 { White is almost lost already. } ) 
e6 $6 { A logical move, though ignoring an immediately winning option. } ( { Instead } 20... a4 $1 { wins on the spot, say after } 21. Rxh7 Nxh7 22. Qh6 e6 23. Qxh7 Kf8 { . } ) 
21. a4 ( { Better was } 21. a3 { though } a4 22. Ba2 Rc6 $1 { ∓ followed by ...Rb6 gives good attacking chances. } ) 
b5 $1 { Opening the position decisively. } ( 21... Bxa4 $5 { ∓ was strong too. } ) 
22. axb5 a4 23. Ba2 ( 23. Bxa4 $4 Nc4 { is all over. } ) 
Bxb5 24. Rxh7 $2 { Desperation. } ( { More stubborn was } 24. Ka1 { though after } Bc4 $1 25. Rb1 Qa3 26. Rb2 { the appearance of White’s position speaks for itself. Black has many promising alternatives such as } Nfd7 27. Rh1 Rb8 28. Rhb1 Rb6 $1 { ∓ keeping White’s pieces completely tied down to the defence. } ) 
Nxh7 25. Qh6 Qxc3+ 26. Kb1 Bc4 $19 { Black has a bulldozing attack while White is unable to even create a serious threat. The rest is simple. } 27. Qxh7+ Kf8 28. Bxc4 ( { Or } 28. Qh8 Ke7 29. Qxc8 { loses to } Bxa2 30. Kxa2 Qxc8 { . } ) 
Rxc4 29. Qh4 { Threatening Qd8-h8#. } Ke8 $1 30. Qf6 Kd7 31. Rh8 Rc8 32. Rxc8 Kxc8 33. Ne2 Qb4+ { White resigns due to unavoidable mate. Final remarks 1. Black’s exchange sacrifice on c3 created a serious distraction, preventing White from focusing on his kingside attack. 2. Once White took on a defensive role to protect the doubled c-pawns, the game was practically won for Black, as there are no threats on the kingside while Black’s queenside play keeps on rolling. White’s queenside is so seriously damaged by the doubled c-pawns that White is unable to organize an effective defence. 3. It is important to note the key role being played by Black’s knight on e5, which covers many essential squares and prevents White’s attack from succeeding. } 
 0-1

